From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ep9
Subject: [PATCH] Apply the ASCII rule of Canonicalize without the u and v
 flags
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Without u or v, JavaScript's Canonicalize (ECMA-262 22.2.2.7.3)
uppercases a character but keeps it as it is when its full uppercase
is not one code unit (U+1F80 -> two units) and when a character at or
above 128 would become ASCII (U+017F long s -> S, U+0131 dotless i ->
I). regress used the simple uppercase mapping for literals and
backreferences, and Unicode simple case folding for classes, \w and
\W. So /ſ/i matched "s", /[a-z]/i and /\w/i matched the Kelvin
sign and long s, /\W/i did not, and /ᾀ/i matched U+1F88. V8 and
the spec say no to each.

canonicalize_legacy implements the spec steps (with a table of the 102
BMP code points whose full uppercase is not one unit, SpecialCasing
17.0). Literals, backreferences and classes (closed under it with
add_icase_code_points_legacy) use it without u or v. With u or v
nothing changes.

The function equals the Canonicalize of node v24.22.0 (Unicode 17.0)
for all 65,536 BMP code units, and so do the classes it makes for a
single character.

Test: tests/legacy_icase.rs.
